Manual review corrections for this page's first pass.

- Bold-vector converter gap (same as the Chapter 3 batch): the source
  MathML wraps direction/basis-vector symbols in an
  mathvariant="bold" mstyle, which extract-exercises.py doesn't detect --
  it either drops the boldness silently (plain i, j, k, u, v) or, where
  the source authored the letter as <mtext> instead of <mi>, emits an
  ad-hoc \text{u}/\text{v}. Fetched the page's raw archive MathML
  directly and confirmed exactly which u/v/i/j/k/E instances are bold in
  the source (every direction vector u, v, unit vectors i, j, k, and the
  electric-intensity vector E in the second-to-last exercise) versus
  which are plain scalars (the velocity components u, v in the final
  fluid-velocity exercise, which are not vectors and are not bold in the
  source). Converted every confirmed-bold instance to \mathbf{...}.
- "(the origin: $(0,0,0))$." trapped the sentence's own closing
  parenthesis inside the math span; moved it back outside to
  "(the origin: $(0,0,0)$)." -- confirmed against the source text, which
  reads "(the origin: (0,0,0))." with the outer paren wrapping the whole
  parenthetical, not just the point.
- "xy-plane" and "x-direction"/"y-direction" were bare prose letters;
  joined into math per this project's $xy$-plane / $x$-axis convention
  used throughout the rest of openstax/.
- "$\sin \pi x\sin 2\pi y$" is ambiguous (repeated "trig-function letter
  letter" structure); disambiguated to $\sin(\pi x)\sin(2\pi y)$,
  matching the parenthesized-argument convention used for \sin(\pi t)
  etc. elsewhere in this project.
- Removed one stray interior space before \end{matrix} in the $v=\langle
  2,-1,2\rangle$ exercise (extractor artifact, no visible effect either
  way but not matching the file's own established spacing).

